A["stage3/B1/g2/o1/combination"] = J({
 "Title": "Episode-calibrated weighted Fisher fusion: learning per-level weights of support p-values on source-modality episodes",
 "Summary": "Compute per-level, per-branch empirical p-values against the target's support normals (inductive, distribution-free) and combine them with weights and a logistic mapping learned on simulated K-shot episodes from the source modalities' labelled validation images.",
 "Hypothesis": "Weighted Fisher fusion with episode-learned level/branch weights improves image AUROC by at least 1 point and reduces expected calibration error by at least 25% relative to unweighted Fisher fusion on held-out BMAD modalities for K >= 4, without any target labels.",
 "Rationale": "Parent 1 (Fisher) gives every level and branch equal weight although the reviews and MVFA's own ablations suggest levels differ in usefulness; parent 2 (episode calibrator) learns from real source abnormal images but on hand-chosen features. Using the eight -log p terms (4 levels x 2 branches) as the calibrator's features keeps Fisher's distribution-free, support-anchored inputs and lets real labelled source data decide how much each level and branch should count. Because the inputs are p-values relative to each episode's own supports, they are on the same scale across modalities, which addresses the reviewers' concern about cross-modality transfer of the calibrator.",
 "Method": "1) Episodes: for each source modality, 200 episodes of K normal supports (valid/good) and 64 balanced validation images. 2) For each image, features x = [mean of top-2% of -log p_bl over pixels] for b in {zero, few}, l in 1..4 (8 features), p from LOO support nulls as in Fisher-MVFA. 3) Logistic regression with non-negative weights (projected gradient), L2 = 1.0, pooled over source episodes. 4) Target: same features with the target supports; probability p = sigma(w^T x + b); threshold at p = 0.5 and at the 95% support-specificity point. 5) Code: train_calibrator.py (episodes), scoring.py fisher_features(); no adapter retraining.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: unweighted Fisher; episode calibrator on raw score features; LOO-Z. Ablations: non-negativity constraint; per-level vs per-branch weights only. Metrics: AUROC, ECE, Brier, sensitivity/specificity at both thresholds; learned weights per held-out fold (interpretability). Falsification: no AUROC or ECE gain over unweighted Fisher.",
 "Provenance": [FED, MLCA]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o5/combination"] = J({
 "Title": "Source-episode selection of the per-image scoring rule: choosing pooling, levels and threshold quantile without target labels",
 "Summary": "Keep the unweighted Fisher p-value statistic but select its few discrete design choices — pooling fraction, set of levels, null pooling — and the threshold quantile by maximising balanced accuracy on simulated K-shot episodes from the source modalities, applying the choices unchanged to the target.",
 "Hypothesis": "Selecting Fisher fusion's pooling fraction, level subset and threshold quantile on source-modality episodes yields realised specificity within 5 points of the 95% target and image AUROC within 0.5 points of the best configuration chosen with target labels (an oracle), whereas fixed defaults deviate by at least 2 AUROC points on at least two held-out modalities.",
 "Rationale": "The reviews of both parents converge on one risk: choices that matter (pooling fraction, which levels, where to set the threshold) are either fixed by hand or learned with many parameters. Parent 2's episode simulation is the principled way to make these choices without target labels, and parent 1's Fisher statistic has only a handful of such choices, so a small discrete search on source episodes is cheap and hard to overfit. This also implements the shared protocol's rule that hyper-parameters are selected on source data, not on the target test set — the very rule MVFA's released code violates (G16).",
 "Method": "1) Search space: pooling top-fraction in {0.5%, 1%, 2%, 5%, 10%, max}; level subsets {all four, deepest two, middle two}; null pooling {per level, all levels}; threshold quantile in {90, 95, 97.5, 99}. 2) Episodes: 200 per source modality (K supports, 64 balanced validation images), scored with Fisher fusion. 3) Objective: mean AUROC over episodes for the scoring choices; then the quantile that brings mean realised specificity closest to 95%. 4) Apply the selected configuration to the held-out modality with its own supports. 5) Oracle reference: same search using target labels (reported only as an upper bound). 6) Code: select_scoring.py; scoring.py parameterised.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: Fisher with defaults (top-2%, all levels); oracle selection; LOO-Z. Metrics: AUROC gap to oracle, realised specificity and sensitivity at the selected quantile, selected configuration per fold. Falsification: AUROC gap to oracle above 1.5 points or specificity deviation above 8 points.",
 "Provenance": [FED, MLCA]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o3/inspiration"] = J({
 "Title": "Nearest-source-modality calibration transfer: calibrating an unseen modality with the source modalities its supports resemble",
 "Summary": "Measure how similar the target's support normals are to each source modality in adapted feature space, and transfer the score-to-probability calibration learned on source episodes with weights proportional to that similarity.",
 "Hypothesis": "Similarity-weighted transfer of source-episode calibrations reduces ECE on the held-out modality by at least 20% relative to a uniformly pooled source calibrator, and the improvement is largest for held-out modalities with a close source neighbour (e.g. LiverCT with BrainMRI as nearest source) and smallest for isolated ones (HIS).",
 "Rationale": "Both parents rely on a reference to decide what a score means — Fisher on the target's supports (normal side only), the episode calibrator on all source modalities equally (both sides). The reviewers questioned whether calibration learned on very different modalities transfers. A different explanation of transfer is similarity: calibration should transfer from source modalities whose normal feature distribution resembles the target's, which can be measured from the support normals alone. This gives a label-free, interpretable rule for how much each source modality should inform the target's calibration.",
 "Method": "1) Per source modality m, fit a calibrator c_m (logistic on Fisher features) from source episodes as in the episode calibrator. 2) Similarity: mean adapted CLS feature of the target supports vs. each source modality's mean normal CLS feature; weights w_m = softmax(-d_m / T), d_m the cosine distance, T selected on source leave-one-source-out episodes. 3) Target probability p = sum_m w_m c_m(x). 4) Code: train_calibrator.py per modality; scoring.py similarity_weights().",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: pooled source calibrator; unweighted Fisher with support threshold; nearest-single-modality calibrator. Metrics: ECE, Brier, AUROC, similarity weights per fold, correlation between nearest-source distance and ECE. Falsification: no ECE gain over pooled calibration.",
 "Provenance": [FED, MLCA]})
A["stage3/B1/g2/o6/combination"] = J({
 "Title": "Fisher p-value fusion with region-mass image scoring and support-derived thresholds",
 "Summary": "Fuse MVFA's levels and branches per pixel with Fisher's statistic over empirical p-values against the support normals, and score each image by its strongest coherent region, so both fusion and pooling are per-image and outlier-robust.",
 "Hypothesis": "Fisher fusion with region-mass pooling keeps image AUROC within 1 point of MVFA's transductive fusion on held-out BMAD modalities (K >= 4), reduces false positives per image at the support threshold by at least 25% relative to Fisher with max pooling, and is at least as stable under test-set subsampling as LOO-Z with region mass.",
 "Rationale": "The two parents fix the two verified defects (transductive fusion; single-pixel max), but parent 1 uses Gaussian-style z-scores (reviewed as a rough tail approximation) while parent 2 uses calibrated p-values with plain top-2% pooling. Taking the stronger fusion (Fisher) and the stronger pooling (region mass) gives a two-component per-image scorer in which every number has a probabilistic meaning: a region is flagged when its pixels are jointly improbable under the support normals.",
 "Method": "1) Null scores per branch/level from support LOO as in Fisher-MVFA; empirical p via searchsorted with floor 1/(N+1). 2) Pixel statistic X = -2 sum log p over 8 terms, upsampled to 240x240. 3) Pixel threshold t = 99th percentile of support LOO X; 8-connected components of X > t; image score = max_c mean(X_c) log(1 + |c|). 4) Image threshold = 95th percentile of support LOO image scores. 5) Code: scoring.py combining fisher_map() and region_mass(); test_few.py per image.",
 "Experiment": PROT_B + " Baselines: transductive MVFA; Fisher + top-2%; LOO-Z + region mass. Metrics: image/pixel AUROC, PRO, FP per image, sensitivity/specificity at thresholds, AUROC std over 20 test subsamples, spike-corruption robustness. Falsification: AUROC loss > 2 points or no FP reduction.",
 "Provenance": [MADCLIP, RECAP, AA, PSMP]})
A["stage3/A1/g2/o3/combination"] = J({
 "Title": "Attribution-proposed, counterfactual-verified pseudo-masks for mask-free segmentation adapters",
 "Summary": "Use gradient attribution of the detection branch's image score to propose candidate anomalous patches cheaply, keep only those whose counterfactual replacement by the nearest normal-bank patch lowers the image score, and train the segmentation adapters on the verified pseudo-masks.",
 "Hypothesis": "Counterfactual verification of attribution proposals raises pseudo-mask precision by at least 0.15 over attribution alone on BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC training images, and segmentation adapters trained on verified pseudo-masks retain at least 80% of mask-supervised MVFA's held-out pixel-AUROC margin over chance.",
 "Rationale": "Parent 2 (attribution) is cheap but noisy, the weakness the reviews noted; parent 1 (counterfactual replacement) measures causal contribution but tests every patch and may miss small lesions whose single-patch replacement barely moves a mean-pooled score. Proposing with attribution and verifying with counterfactuals keeps the causal test where it matters and allows replacing groups of proposed patches together, which makes small multi-patch lesions detectable.",
 "Method": "1) Detection branch trained with image labels (unchanged). 2) Proposals: top 10% patches by gradient x input attribution at >= 2 of 4 levels. 3) Verification: replace each connected group of proposed patches by nearest normal-bank tokens (10% coreset of the same dataset's normal images) and compute the drop in image score; keep groups with drop above the 90th percentile of drops for random groups of equal size. 4) Pseudo-mask = verified groups (foreground-restricted, upsampled). 5) Train segmentation adapters with Focal + Dice on pseudo-masks; normal images empty. 6) Code: make_verified_masks.py, train_zero.py.",
 "Experiment": PROT_A + " Baselines: mask-supervised MVFA; attribution only; counterfactual only; asymmetric MIL. Ablations: proposal fraction; group vs single-patch replacement; verification percentile. Metrics: pseudo-mask precision/recall/IoU (analysis), held-out pixel AUROC, PRO, image AUROC. Falsification: precision gain below 0.05.",
 "Provenance": [MLCA, MADCLIP, RECAP]})
